# Line endings, declared rather than left to each machine's git config.
#
# Every generated file in this repository is checked byte for byte against what
# its builder produces, and several tests compare a generated page to a committed
# fixture. On a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true, git hands those files
# to Node with CRLF line endings, the comparison fails on whitespace alone, and
# the failure reads as content drift in a page nobody touched. Declaring the
# endings here means the working tree matches the blob on every platform, so a
# fresh clone verifies the same way everywhere.
#
# No normalization diff is expected from adding this: nothing committed here
# carried CRLF when it was written (2026-09-14).

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell and hooks must be LF regardless of anything above; a CRLF shebang line
# is not executable.
*.sh            text eol=lf
.githooks/*     text eol=lf

# Binary, so git never rewrites a byte of them. An image with a mangled newline
# fails validate.js's magic-byte check, which is the right outcome but a
# confusing way to discover a line-ending setting.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.webp          binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary
